.TH CTIME 3
.SH NAME
ctime, localtime, gmtime, asctime, timezone, clock, difftime, mktime, strftime
\-  convert date and time to ASCII
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <time.h>
.PP
.B char *ctime(const time_t *clock);
.PP
.B struct tm *localtime(const time_t *clock);
.PP
.B struct tm *gmtime(const time_t *clock);
.PP
.B char *asctime(const struct tm *tm);
.PP
.B char *timezone(int zone, int dst);
.PP
.B clock_t clock(void);
.PP
.B double difftime(time_t end, time_t start);
.PP
.B time_t mktime(struct tm *tp);
.PP
.B size_t strftime(char *s, size_t n, const char *fmt, const struct tm *tp);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ctime
converts a time pointed to by
.I clock
such as returned by
.IR time (2)
into ASCII
and returns a pointer to a
26-character string
in the following form.
All the fields have constant width.
.PP
    Sun Sep 16 01:03:52 1973\\n\\0
.PP
.I Localtime
and
.I gmtime
return pointers to structures containing
the broken-down time.
.I Localtime
corrects for the time zone and possible daylight savings time;
.I gmtime
converts directly to GMT, which is the time UNIX uses.
.I Asctime
converts a broken-down time to ASCII and returns a pointer
to a 26-character string.
.PP
The structure declaration from the include file is:
.RS
.PP
.nf
struct tm {
    int tm_sec;
    int tm_min;
    int tm_hour;
    int tm_mday;
    int tm_mon;
    int tm_year;
    int tm_wday;
    int tm_yday;
    int tm_isdst;
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page pulled this in with a
.B .so
of the installed header; it is written out here, there being no /usr/include on
this system.  <time.h> also declares every routine now.  v7's held the struct
alone and left the calls to K&R's implicit
.BR int .
.PP
These quantities give the time on a 24-hour clock,
day of month (1-31), month of year (0-11), day of week
(Sunday = 0), year \- 1900, day of year (0-365),
and a flag that is nonzero if daylight saving time is in effect.
.PP
When local time is called for,
the program consults the system to determine the time zone and
whether the standard U.S.A. daylight saving time adjustment is
appropriate.
The program knows about the peculiarities
of this conversion in 1974 and 1975;
if necessary,
a table for these years can be extended.
.B Note:
the zone comes from
.IR ftime (2),
as it did in v7 \- the kernel owns the westward offset and the daylight flag, and
there is no zoneinfo to read.
.PP
.I Timezone
returns the name of the time zone associated with its first argument,
which is measured in minutes westward from Greenwich.
If the second argument is 0, the standard name is used,
otherwise the Daylight Saving version.
If the required name does not appear in a table
built into the routine,
the difference from GMT is produced; e.g.
in Afghanistan
.I timezone(\-(60*4+30), 0)
is appropriate because it is 4:30 ahead of GMT
and the string
.B GMT+4:30
is produced.
.B Note:
.I timezone
is declared by no header, C11 having
.B %Z
instead; a caller that wants the v7 spelling declares it itself.  So does
.IR dysize ().
.PP
.B Note:
the four that follow are C11 and had no v7 ancestor.
.PP
.I Clock
returns the processor time used, in units of
.BR CLOCKS_PER_SEC ,
or (clock_t)\-1 if it is not available.
.PP
.I Difftime
returns the difference of two times in seconds, as a
.BR double .
.PP
.I Mktime
is the inverse of
.IR localtime :
it takes a broken-down local time, normalises its fields, fills in
.I tm_wday
and
.IR tm_yday ,
and returns the corresponding
.BR time_t ,
or (time_t)\-1 if it cannot be represented.
.PP
.I Strftime
formats a broken-down time into
.I s
according to
.IR fmt ,
writing at most
.I n
characters, and returns the number written not counting the terminating null \-
or 0 if the result would not fit.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
time(2), ftime(2)
.SH BUGS
The return values point to static data
whose content is overwritten by each call.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS time_t is one word, and it reaches past 2100
41 bits of seconds, so there is
.B no 2038 problem
\- and that is not merely reassuring, because it breaks a v7 assumption.
.PP
.B dysize() uses the full Gregorian rule now.
v7 wrote
.B "(y % 4) == 0"
and was right until 2100, which a two-digit year could not reach and a 41-bit
.B time_t
can.
.PP
The same collapse is why
.IR stime (2)
sheds an argument that v7's gate carried: v7's
.B time_t
was two PDP-11 words.
.SS CLOCKS_PER_SEC is HZ
250, the tick this kernel programs.
.I Clock
can be no finer than the clock the machine has.
.PP
.B Note:
under b6sim the counters are the
.I host's
clock ticks, whose rate is not this machine's HZ.  A program that divides by
CLOCKS_PER_SEC gets a different answer in the two harnesses, which is why the
test converts only literal
.B time_t
values.
.SS Two more departures from v7's arithmetic
.I Gmtime
and
.I asctime
.B name their fields.
v7 walked
.B struct tm
through an
.BR "int *" ,
filling it positionally.  That works here only because every field is one word,
and it is not worth relying on.
.PP
.B February is not patched in place.
v7 wrote 29 into its static month-length table for the duration of a leap year
and wrote 28 back afterwards \- a static mutated and restored, which any early
return leaves wrong.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/ctime.c, clock.c, difftime.c, mktime.c, strftime.c, timezone.c
